inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>ISHIZU, FUMIO</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>ITOH, KENJI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KATSURA, TAKATOSHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MATSUNAMI, YOSHINORI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MOCHIZUKI, MITSURU</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NAGANO, HIROAKI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SHIMOZAWA, MITSUHIRO</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A cellular telephone comprises a direct conversion receiver circuit (14) using even-harmonic quadrature mixer (24), and high-pass filters (56, 58, 64, 66) for removing DC offset components before and behind variable-gain amplifiers (60, 62) in a baseband circuit (30). The DC components appearing in the even-harmonic quadrature mixer (24) is constant, while the DC components appearing in the variable-gain amplifiers (60, 62) change with time. Therefore, the cutoff frequency of the high-pass filters (64, 66) in the back stage is set higher than the cutoff frequency (fc1) of the high-pass filters (56, 58) in the front stage so that the transient response may be fast.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>Cette invention se rapporte à un téléphone cellulaire, qui comprend un circuit récepteur de conversion directe (14) utilisant un mélangeur en quadrature à harmonique pair (24), et des filtres passe-haut (56, 58, 64, 66) destinés à éliminer les composantes de décalage CC devant et derrière des amplificateurs de gain variable (60, 62) dans un circuit bande de base (30). Les composantes CC apparaissant dans le mélangeur en quadrature à harmonique pair (24) sont constantes, tandis que les composantes CC apparaissant dans les amplificateurs de gain variable (60, 62) changent avec le temps. Par conséquent, la fréquence de coupure des filtres passe-haut (64, 66) dans l'étage arrière est réglée à un niveau supérieur à la fréquence de coupure (fc1) des filtres passe-haut (56, 58) dans l'étage avant, pour que la réponse transitoire puisse être rapide.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>